  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Showers and scattered thunderstorms expected again \ntoday, then drier and less humid Sunday. \n\nA soupy airmass (humid, dwpts 60s to near 70F) remain over the Lower \nGreat Lakes and temps in the 60s to around 70F. The next feature of \ninterest is a mid-level convectively augment shortwave tracking \nthrough the base of the broad trough. Latest CAMs show the \nconvection over Ohio expanding east and northeast and arriving \nbetween 10Z-12Z across far Western New York, then working east \nacross the region. Additional chances for showers and storms will \nthen be possible this afternoon, especially along lake breeze \nboundaries as MLCAPE values ramp up to +2500 j/kg. Given the soupy \nairmass (PW 1.5\"-1.7\") heavy rainfall will 'likely' be the main \nthreat which could cause some localized issues. The threat for \nsevere weather still appears low at this time. As was noted, bulk \nshear values remain on the low side (25 knots). That said, a few \nstorms still could produce strong gusty winds.  \n\nAs we work though the evening hours the weak cold front will arrive \nbringing the potential for some more storms as it crosses the \nregion. After that...we will see a slow but gradual drying trend \novernight. A weak surface ridge crosses the region Sunday which will \nprovide mainly dry weather and overall comfortable humidity level. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather returns for the Lower Great Lakes \nto start the new work week.\n\nA zonal flow early next week will begin to shift southward, with a \nmore noticeable dip late in the week as a deepening upper level low \ndrops southward from Canada. \n\nA moisture rich airmass will remain through the beginning of next \nweek, one that will support not only afternoon convection, but the \npossibility of overnight convection, though exact timing of subtle \nshortwave troughs is with still great uncertainty at this point in \ntime.",
